WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.320
All right.

00:00.320 --> 00:01.640
So now we come to the big question.

00:01.640 --> 00:03.160
How are we going to build the gold layer.

00:03.200 --> 00:05.200
As usual we start with analyzing.

00:05.200 --> 00:10.840
So now what we're going to do here is to explore and understand what are the main business objects that

00:10.840 --> 00:12.640
are hidden inside our source system.

00:12.640 --> 00:14.720
So as you can see we have two sources six files.

00:14.720 --> 00:17.200
And here we have to identify what are the business objects.

00:17.200 --> 00:20.280
Once we have this understanding then we can start coding.

00:20.280 --> 00:23.800
And here the main transformation that we are doing is data integration.

00:23.800 --> 00:26.280
And here usually I split it into three steps.

00:26.280 --> 00:30.800
The first one we're going to go and build those business objects that we have identified.

00:30.800 --> 00:36.120
And after we have a business objects, we have to look at it and decide what is the type of this table?

00:36.120 --> 00:37.200
Is it a dimension?

00:37.200 --> 00:40.360
Is it a fact or is it like maybe a flat table?

00:40.360 --> 00:42.320
So what type of table that we have built?

00:42.320 --> 00:47.800
And the last step is of course we have now to rename all the columns into something friendly and easy

00:47.800 --> 00:51.600
to understand so that our consumers don't struggle with technical names.

00:51.600 --> 00:55.320
So once we have all those steps, what we're going to do, it's time to validate what we have created.

00:55.320 --> 00:59.720
So what we have to do, the new data model that we have created, it should be connectable.

00:59.720 --> 01:03.130
And we have to check that the data integration is done correctly.

01:03.130 --> 01:06.210
And once everything is fine, we cannot skip the last step.

01:06.210 --> 01:09.490
We have to document and as well commit our work in the git.

01:09.490 --> 01:12.370
And he will be introducing new type of documentations.

01:12.370 --> 01:14.970
So we're gonna have a diagram about the data model.

01:14.970 --> 01:18.890
We're going to build a data dictionary where we're going to describe the data model.

01:18.890 --> 01:21.330
And of course we can extend the data flow diagram.

01:21.330 --> 01:22.650
So this is our process.

01:22.650 --> 01:26.490
Those are the main steps that we will do in order to build the gold layer.

01:30.570 --> 01:30.930
Okay.

01:30.930 --> 01:32.730
So what is exactly data modeling.

01:32.730 --> 01:39.690
Usually the source system can deliver for you raw data and organized messy not very useful in its current

01:39.690 --> 01:40.250
state.

01:40.250 --> 01:46.370
But now the data modeling is the process of taking this raw data and then organize it and structure

01:46.410 --> 01:48.170
it in meaningful way.

01:48.170 --> 01:54.290
So what we are doing, we are putting the data in a new, friendly and easy to understand objects like

01:54.290 --> 01:56.370
customers, orders, products.

01:56.410 --> 01:59.010
Each one of them is focused on specific information.

01:59.010 --> 02:04.010
And what is very important is we're going to describe the relationship between those objects.

02:04.010 --> 02:06.250
So by connecting them using lines.

02:06.250 --> 02:09.570
So what we have built on the right side we call it logical data model.

02:09.570 --> 02:13.930
If you compare it to the left side you can see the data model makes it really easy to understand our

02:13.930 --> 02:16.730
data and the relationship the processes behind them.

02:16.730 --> 02:21.690
Now in data modeling we have three different stages or let's say three different ways on how to draw

02:21.690 --> 02:22.490
a data model.

02:22.490 --> 02:25.130
The first stage is the conceptual data model.

02:25.130 --> 02:27.690
Here the focus is only on the entity.

02:27.690 --> 02:31.690
So we have customers orders products and we don't go in details at all.

02:31.690 --> 02:35.210
So we don't specify any columns or attributes inside those boxes.

02:35.210 --> 02:40.650
We just want to focus what are the entities that we have and as well the relationship between them.

02:40.650 --> 02:44.290
So the conceptual data model don't focus at all on the details.

02:44.290 --> 02:46.090
It just gives the big picture.

02:46.090 --> 02:49.970
So the second data model that we can build is the logical data model.

02:49.970 --> 02:55.290
And here we start specifying what are the different columns that we can find in each entity.

02:55.290 --> 02:58.570
Like we have the customer ID, the first name, last name and so on.

02:58.570 --> 03:01.300
And we still draw the relationship between those entities.

03:01.300 --> 03:04.660
And as well, we make it clear which columns are the primary key and so on.

03:04.660 --> 03:06.540
So as you can see, we have here more details.

03:06.540 --> 03:10.020
But one thing we don't describe a lot of details for each column.

03:10.020 --> 03:15.100
And we are not to worry how exactly we can store those tables in the database.

03:15.140 --> 03:18.220
The third and last stage we have the physical data model.

03:18.220 --> 03:22.180
This is where everything gets ready before creating it in the database.

03:22.180 --> 03:27.540
So here you have to add all the technical details like adding for each column, the data types and the

03:27.540 --> 03:32.180
links of each data type, and many other database techniques and details.

03:32.180 --> 03:35.860
So again, if you look to the conceptual data model, it gives us the big picture.

03:35.860 --> 03:39.900
And in the logical data model, we dive into details of what data we need.

03:39.940 --> 03:44.980
And the physical layer model prepares everything for the implementation in the database.

03:44.980 --> 03:50.780
And to be honest, in my projects I only draw the conceptual and the logical data model because drawing

03:50.780 --> 03:54.460
and building the physical data model needs a lot of efforts and time.

03:54.460 --> 03:59.260
And there are many tools like in Databricks, they automatically Generate those models.

03:59.300 --> 04:03.740
So in this project, what we're going to do, we're going to draw the logical data model for the gold

04:03.740 --> 04:04.180
layer.

04:08.220 --> 04:13.100
All right so now for analytics and especially for data warehousing and business intelligence we need

04:13.140 --> 04:17.300
a special data model that is optimized for reporting and analytics.

04:17.300 --> 04:21.140
And it should be flexible, scalable and as well easy to understand.

04:21.140 --> 04:23.380
And for that we have two special data models.

04:23.380 --> 04:25.940
The first type of data model we have the star schema.

04:25.940 --> 04:30.140
It has a central fact table in the middle and surrounded by dimensions.

04:30.180 --> 04:36.260
The fact table contains transactions, events and the dimensions contains descriptive informations and

04:36.260 --> 04:41.500
the relationship between the fact table and the middle and the dimensions around it forms like a star

04:41.500 --> 04:41.980
shape.

04:41.980 --> 04:43.940
And that's why we call it star schema.

04:43.940 --> 04:47.140
And we have another data model called snowflake schema.

04:47.180 --> 04:49.180
It looks very similar to the star schema.

04:49.180 --> 04:52.740
So we have again the fact in the middle and surrounded by dimensions.

04:52.740 --> 04:59.310
But the big difference is that we break the dimensions into smaller sub dimensions and the shape of

04:59.310 --> 05:00.310
this data model.

05:00.350 --> 05:04.110
As you are extending the dimensions, it's going to look like a snowflake.

05:04.150 --> 05:08.270
So now if you compare them side by side, you can see that the star schema looks easier, right?

05:08.310 --> 05:11.470
So it is usually easy to understand easy to query.

05:11.510 --> 05:13.310
It is really perfect for analysis.

05:13.310 --> 05:15.110
But it has one issue with that.

05:15.110 --> 05:19.950
The dimension might contain duplicates and your dimensions get bigger with time.

05:19.950 --> 05:24.070
Now if you compare it to the snowflake, you can see the schema is more complex you.

05:24.070 --> 05:28.790
So you need a lot of knowledge and efforts in order to query something from the snowflake.

05:28.790 --> 05:31.510
But the main advantage here comes with the normalization.

05:31.510 --> 05:36.950
As you are breaking those redundancies in small tables, you can optimize the storage, but to be honest,

05:36.990 --> 05:38.070
who care about the storage.

05:38.070 --> 05:43.350
So for this project, I have choose to use the star schema because it is very commonly used perfect

05:43.350 --> 05:44.270
for reporting.

05:44.270 --> 05:48.710
Like for example, if you are using power BI and we don't have to worry about the storage.

05:48.710 --> 05:52.430
So that's why we can adapt this model to build our gold layer.

05:56.510 --> 06:01.470
Okay, so now one more thing about those data models is that they contain two types of tables fact and

06:01.470 --> 06:02.230
dimensions.

06:02.230 --> 06:08.030
So when I say this is a fact table or a dimension table, well the dimension contains descriptive informations

06:08.030 --> 06:11.310
or like categories that gives some context to your data.

06:11.310 --> 06:16.110
For example a product info, you have product name, category subcategories and so on.

06:16.150 --> 06:21.110
This is like a table that is describing the products and this we call it dimension.

06:21.110 --> 06:22.830
But in the other hand we have facts.

06:22.830 --> 06:25.110
They are events like transactions.

06:25.110 --> 06:27.310
They contain three important informations.

06:27.350 --> 06:31.230
First you have multiple ideas from multiple dimensions.

06:31.230 --> 06:36.590
Then we have like date informations like when the transaction or the event did happen.

06:36.590 --> 06:40.270
And the third type of information you're gonna have like measures and numbers.

06:40.270 --> 06:44.830
So if you see those three types of data in one table then this is a fact.

06:44.830 --> 06:49.510
So if you have a table that answers how much or how many then this is a facts.

06:49.510 --> 06:54.710
But if you have a table that answers who what where then this is a dimension table.

06:54.710 --> 06:57.150
So this is what dimension and fact tables.
